## 10. Hashing Basics

### What is a Hash Function?
A hash function takes an input of **any size** and produces a **fixed-size output** (the hash/digest).

Key properties:
- **One-way** — impossible to reverse the output back to the input
- **Deterministic** — same input always produces the same output
- **Avalanche effect** — even a single bit change in the input causes a completely different output
- **Fast to compute** but slow to reverse
- No key is involved (unlike encryption)

> Hashing is mainly used for **integrity verification** and **secure password storage**.

### Common Hash Algorithms

| Algorithm | Full Name | Notes |
|---|---|---|
| **MD5** | Message Digest 5 | Fast but broken; not recommended for security |
| **SHA-1** | Secure Hash Algorithm 1 | Deprecated; collision vulnerabilities found |
| **SHA-256** | Secure Hash Algorithm 256-bit | Current standard; widely used |
| **NTLM** | NT LAN Manager | Used for Windows authentication |

### Hash Collisions
A **collision** occurs when two different inputs produce the same hash output. This is caused by the **pigeonhole effect** — there are infinite possible inputs but a finite number of possible hash outputs. This is why older algorithms like MD5 and SHA-1 are no longer considered secure.

