## 11. Password Storage & Salting

### Insecure Password Storage Practices
- Storing passwords in **plaintext**
- Using **deprecated encryption** algorithms
- Using **weak/insecure hashing** algorithms (e.g. MD5 without salt)

### How Secure Password Hashing Works
1. User sets a password
2. System hashes the password and stores only the hash
3. On login, the system hashes the submitted password and compares it to the stored hash
4. **Passwords are never stored or transmitted in plaintext**

### Password Salting
A **salt** is a random value added to a password before hashing — making every hash unique even if two users have the same password.

```
hash(password + salt) → stored hash
```

- Salts are **not secret** — they are stored alongside the hash
- Salting defeats **rainbow table attacks** (pre-computed hash databases)
- Tools like **bcrypt** and **scrypt** handle salting automatically

### Rainbow Tables
Pre-computed databases of hash → password mappings used to crack unsalted hashes quickly.

**Online tools for cracking unsalted hashes:**
- [CrackStation](https://crackstation.net)
- [Hashes.com](https://hashes.com)

### Secure Hashing Algorithms for Passwords

| Algorithm | Notes |
|---|---|
| **bcrypt** | Widely used; slow by design to resist brute force |
| **scrypt** | Memory-hard; resistant to GPU attacks |
| **Argon2** | Winner of the Password Hashing Competition; highly recommended |

