## 14. Password Cracking Tools

### John the Ripper

A powerful open-source password cracking tool supporting many hash formats.

**Basic syntax:**
```bash
john [options] <hash_file>
```

**Common options:**

| Option | Purpose |
|---|---|
| `--wordlist=<path>` | Use a wordlist for dictionary attack |
| `--format=<format>` | Specify the hash format (e.g. `--format=md5`) |
| `--format=NT` | Used for Windows NTLM passwords |

**Example:**
```bash
john --wordlist=/usr/share/wordlists/rockyou.txt hashes.txt
john --wordlist=wordlist.txt --format=md5 hashes.txt
```

### Hashcat

An alternative GPU-accelerated password cracking tool. Requires specifying a **hash mode number** instead of a format name.

```bash
hashcat -m <mode> <hash_file> <wordlist>
```

> Common mode numbers: `0` = MD5, `1000` = NTLM, `1800` = SHA-512, `3200` = bcrypt

---

## 15. John the Ripper — Advanced Usage

### unshadow
Combines `/etc/passwd` (user account info) and `/etc/shadow` (hashed passwords) into a single crackable file.

```bash
unshadow /etc/passwd /etc/shadow > unshadowed.txt
john unshadowed.txt
```

### Single Crack Mode
Uses account information (like username) to generate password guesses — e.g. if the username is `alice`, it tries `alice123`, `alice321`, `Alice1`, etc.

```bash
john --single hashes.txt
```

### Custom / Complex Passwords (Polo Password)
John can be configured with rules to generate passwords that meet complexity requirements — uppercase, lowercase, numbers, special characters — useful for cracking passwords with known policies.

---

## 16. Cracking Specific File Types

### zip2john — Cracking ZIP Files
Converts a password-protected ZIP file into a crackable hash format.

```bash
zip2john [options] <zipfile> > output.txt
john output.txt
```

### rar2john — Cracking RAR Files
Same process for RAR archives — convert to hash, then crack with John.

```bash
rar2john <rarfile> > output.txt
john output.txt
```

### ssh2john — Cracking SSH Private Keys
Converts an encrypted SSH private key (`id_rsa`) into a crackable hash format.

```bash
ssh2john id_rsa > id_rsa.hash
john id_rsa.hash
```

